✅ 1. MUnit for Parallel For Each
🔹 Challenge
Parallel execution → no guaranteed order
You cannot assert sequence
Need to mock internal processors
✅ Strategy
Mock the processor inside the Parallel For Each
Validate:
Each item is processed
Payload size / attributes
Avoid checking execution order
✅ Example Mule Flow



XML
<parallel-foreach doc:name="Parallel For Each">
    <flow-ref name="childFlow"/>
</parallel-foreach>
✅ MUnit Test



XML
<munit:test name="parallelForEachTest">
    <munit:behavior>
        <munit-tools:mock-when processor="flow-ref">
            <munit-tools:with-attributes>
                <munit-tools:with-attribute attributeName="name" value="childFlow"/>
            </munit-tools:with-attributes>
            <munit-tools:then-return payload="#['mocked']"/>
        </munit-tools:mock-when>
    </munit:behavior>
 
    <munit:execution>
        <flow-ref name="mainFlow"/>
    </munit:execution>
 
    <munit:validation>
        <munit-tools:verify-call processor="flow-ref" times="3"/>
    </munit:validation>
</munit:test>

Show more lines
✅ Key Assertion: Number of executions, not order

🔹 Interview Tip
“In parallel for each, MUnit assertions should validate execution count and outcomes, not payload order.”

✅ 2. MUnit for Scatter‑Gather
🔹 Challenge
Multiple routes executed in parallel
Output is a list of route responses
Results may come in any order
✅ Strategy
Mock each route
Assert:
Response size
Presence of expected values
Avoid index‑based assertions
✅ Example Mule Flow



XML
<scatter-gather>
    <route>
        <flow-ref name="route1"/>
    </route>
    <route>
        <flow-ref name="route2"/>
    </route>
</scatter-gather>
✅ MUnit Test



XML
<munit:test name="scatterGatherTest">
    <munit:behavior>
        <munit-tools:mock-when processor="flow-ref">
            <munit-tools:with-attributes>
                <munit-tools:with-attribute attributeName="name" value="route1"/>
            </munit-tools:with-attributes>
            <munit-tools:then-return payload="route1Response"/>
        </munit-tools:mock-when>
 
        <munit-tools:mock-when processor="flow-ref">
            <munit-tools:with-attributes>
                <munit-tools:with-attribute attributeName="name" value="route2"/>
            </munit-tools:with-attributes>
            <munit-tools:then-return payload="route2Response"/>
        </munit-tools:mock-when>
    </munit:behavior>
 
    <munit:execution>
        <flow-ref name="mainFlow"/>
    </munit:execution>
 
    <munit:validation>
        <munit-tools:assert-that expression="#[payload]"
            is="#[contains('route1Response') and contains('route2Response')]"/>
    </munit:validation>
</munit:test>

Show more lines
🔹 Interview Tip
“Scatter‑gather returns a collection of responses; MUnit tests should assert response presence, not route execution order.”

