Copilot said:
Circuit Breaker is a design pattern used to protect your Mule application from repeatedly calling a failing downstream system (API, Database, Salesforce, SAP, etc.).

Think of it like an electrical circuit breaker:

Normal state → requests flow normally ✅
Too many failures → circuit opens ❌
Requests are blocked temporarily 🛑
After some time → a test request is allowed 🔄
If successful → circuit closes again ✅
Why Do We Need a Circuit Breaker?
Scenario
Your Mule API calls an external Order Management System.




Plain Text
Client
  ↓
Mule API
  ↓
Order API (Down)
Without Circuit Breaker:

1000 requests come in
Mule tries all 1000 calls
All fail
Threads get occupied
Response times increase
Entire application becomes unstable
With Circuit Breaker:




Plain Text
Failure 1
Failure 2
Failure 3
↓
Circuit Opens
↓
Further requests blocked immediately
↓
Fast failure response returned
This protects Mule resources.

Circuit Breaker States
1. Closed State (Normal)



Plain Text
Request → API → Response
Everything works
Requests pass normally
Failures are monitored
2. Open State



Plain Text
Request
   ↓
Circuit Open
   ↓
Immediate Failure
Downstream system is unhealthy
No calls are sent
Saves threads and resources
3. Half-Open State
After a wait period:




Plain Text
Allow 1 Request
        ↓
Success?
Success



Plain Text
Half Open
    ↓
Close Circuit
Failure



Plain Text
Half Open
    ↓
Open Again
MuleSoft Real-Life Scenario
Scenario
Process API calls Salesforce.




Plain Text
Experience API
   ↓
Process API
   ↓
Salesforce
Salesforce starts returning:




JSON
503 Service Unavailable
Without Circuit Breaker:

Every request continues hitting Salesforce
API response time increases
Worker threads get exhausted
With Circuit Breaker:

After threshold is reached:




JSON
{
  "status": "Service Temporarily Unavailable"
}
is returned immediately.

Does MuleSoft Have a Built-in Circuit Breaker?
Interview Answer
MuleSoft does not provide a dedicated Circuit Breaker component like Spring Cloud Circuit Breaker, Hystrix, or Resilience4j.

Instead, we implement similar behavior using:

Until Successful
Caching/Object Store
Custom failure counters
API Manager policies
Messaging queues
External resiliency frameworks
Common MuleSoft Implementation
Option 1: Object Store Based Circuit Breaker
Store failure count:




Plain Text
Failure Count = 5
When threshold reached:




DataWeave
if(vars.failureCount > 5)
   "Circuit Open"
else
   Call Backend
Store state in:




Plain Text
Object Store
Option 2: Queue-Based Approach



Plain Text
Mule API
   ↓
MQ/JMS
   ↓
Consumer
   ↓
Backend
If backend fails:

Messages remain in queue
System doesn't crash
Traffic is buffered
Option 3: Retry + Circuit Breaker



Plain Text
Try Backend
   ↓
Retry 3 Times
   ↓
Failure
   ↓
Open Circuit
Very common enterprise design.

Interview Scenario Questions
Q1. External API is down. How do you prevent continuous failures?
Answer
I would implement a Circuit Breaker pattern. After a predefined failure threshold, requests are blocked temporarily and a fallback response is returned. This prevents thread exhaustion and protects downstream systems.

Q2. Can we implement Circuit Breaker using Until Successful?
Answer
Partially. Until Successful provides retry capability, but it is not a true Circuit Breaker. A complete Circuit Breaker requires tracking failures and temporarily stopping requests.

Q3. Circuit Breaker vs Retry
Retry	Circuit Breaker
Tries again	Stops calling
Handles transient issues	Handles prolonged failures
Increases traffic	Reduces traffic
Short-term recovery	System protection
Q4. Circuit Breaker vs Rate Limiting
Circuit Breaker	Rate Limiting
Protects failing backend	Controls client traffic
Based on failures	Based on request count
Runtime health check	Traffic governance
